ratorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186248072 1145 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-csc⁡x+C \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =tan⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x-2csc⁡xcot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =tan⁡x-2(-csc⁡x)+C \\\\  & =tan⁡x+2csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534549 false] [17592186256857 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274243 13194139534549 false] [17592186256857 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294207 13194139534549 true] [17592186257116 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274310 13194139534549 false] [17592186257116 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294220 13194139534549 true] [17592186257116 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534549 false] [17592186257116 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/d624edaffd8c90de40041d419cf65bcf8f3af5d31c8d88d1123bd8f3d7555166.png)\n\nWhat is the length of the major axis of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534549 true] [17592186257134 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274314 13194139534549 false] [17592186257134 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294226 13194139534549 true] [17592186257386 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274359 13194139534549 false] [17592186257386 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294232 13194139534549 true] [17592186257386 1100 "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1$" 13194139534549 false] [17592186257386 1100 "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186257386 1145 "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}x={sin}^{2}x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534549 false] [17592186257386 1145 "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186257802 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274431 13194139534549 false] [17592186257802 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294252 13194139534549 true] [17592186258090 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274463 13194139534549 false] [17592186258090 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294265 13194139534549 true] [17592186258090 1100 "$\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" 13194139534549 false] [17592186258090 1100 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186258090 1145 "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534549 false] [17592186258090 1145 "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186258842 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274560 13194139534549 false] [17592186258842 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294292 13194139534549 true] [17592186258842 1145 "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{'}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6x(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=6x(x-1)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ec7ea6baa82d60a6cf472c904460190042d889d86cb092b19d9809111bd93b36.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" 13194139534549 false] [17592186258842 1145 "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6x(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=6x(x-1)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035241/assets/ec7ea6baa82d60a6cf472c904460190042d889d86cb092b19d9809111bd93b36.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186260204 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274668 13194139534549 false] [17592186260204 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294321 13194139534549 true] [17592186260869 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274816 13194139534549 false] [17592186260869 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294348 13194139534549 true] [17592186260869 1100 "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" 13194139534549 false] [17592186260869 1100 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186260869 1145 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534549 false] [17592186260869 1145 "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186261501 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274968 13194139534549 false] [17592186261501 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294361 13194139534549 true] [17592186261529 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274974 13194139534549 false] [17592186261529 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294367 13194139534549 true] [17592186261529 1145 "Let's introduce the new variable $u={2}^{z}-9.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}={2}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}\\,\\text{d}u={2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\cdot {2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}{\\operatorname{ln}2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534549 false] [17592186261529 1145 "Let's introduce the new variable $u={2}^{z}-9.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}={2}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}u={2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\cdot {2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186261574 1017 #edb/ref 17592186261575 13194139534549 false] [17592186261574 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294373 13194139534549 true] [17592186261704 1017 #edb/ref 17592186275019 13194139534549 false] [17592186261704 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294393 13194139534549 true] [17592186294186 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294187 13194139534549 true] [17592186294186 1089 "q-101417" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294186 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534549 true] [17592186294186 1100 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $u⊥v$ and $x<0$, find the value of $x.$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294186 1113 #uuid "3893bb9a-24bf-5f2a-8cb4-667c5be755d4" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294186 1145 "We know that $u⊥v$ ($u$ and $v$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-2)+2x\\cdot x \\\\  & =-18+2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -18+2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $x<0.$ Therefore, $x=-3.$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294188 13194139534549 true] [17592186294187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294189 13194139534549 true] [17592186294187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294190 13194139534549 true] [17592186294187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294191 13194139534549 true] [17592186294187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294192 13194139534549 true] [17592186294187 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534549 true] [17592186294187 1041 "selection" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294187 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294190 13194139534549 true] [17592186294187 1110 #uuid "92fbf9d8-c1e1-5ee1-9dcd-80f58cd34bf8" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294188 1034 "-5" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534549 true] [17592186294188 1111 #uuid "f2988f4d-c60d-5c79-8a12-3bc7ecb9b924" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294189 1034 "-4" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294189 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534549 true] [17592186294189 1111 #uuid "fc0d2878-365e-5c00-8eb8-e6c896cd2249" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294190 1034 "-3" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534549 true] [17592186294190 1111 #uuid "f08499b8-d38b-557d-83fb-451ce3eac732" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294191 1034 "-1" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294191 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534549 true] [17592186294191 1111 #uuid "ffd71fc7-7dd0-594b-9a9e-18b3c358d8f7" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294192 1034 "-2" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294192 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534549 true] [17592186294192 1111 #uuid "b3ac1cc3-16ca-5657-b07f-633d638bc2ea" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294193 1017 #edb/ref 17592186294194 13194139534549 true] [17592186294193 1089 "q-107654" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294193 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534549 true] [17592186294193 1100 "Express $S=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{5}{6}+⋯+\\frac{5}{26}+\\frac{5}{28}$ in sigma notation." 13194139534549 true] [17592186294193 1113 #uuid "eb06b6e8-09c0-5357-8229-6e5ebd9caeb6" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294193 1145 "We first examine the numerators and denominators of the corresponding sequence.\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{4},\\,\\frac{5}{6},\\,…,\\,\\frac{5}{26},\\,\\frac{5}{28}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find a pattern in the terms. To do this, we note the following:\n\n- The numerators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n5,\\,5,\\,5,\\,…,\\,5,\\,5\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5.\n$$\n\n- The denominators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n2,\\,4,\\,…,\\,26,\\,28\n$$\n\nThese values are multiples of $2,$ and since $28÷2=14,$ there are $14$ terms in total. The $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=2n,\\,1\\le n\\le 14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{4},\\,\\frac{5}{6},\\,…,\\,\\frac{5}{26},\\,\\frac{5}{28}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}=\\frac{5}{2n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=14.$ \n\nTherefore, we can express our series using sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{14}\\frac{5}{2n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294195 13194139534549 true] [17592186294194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294196 13194139534549 true] [17592186294194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294197 13194139534549 true] [17592186294194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294198 13194139534549 true] [17592186294194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186294199 13194139534549 true] [17592186294194 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534549 true] [17592186294194 1041 "selection" 13194139534549 true] [17592186294194 1104 #edb/ref 17592186294197 13194139534549 true] [17592186294194 1110 #uuid "9f252